  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1204 PM CDT Fri Aug 21 2026\n\nWV imagery indicates a northwest flow aloft prevailing across \nthe Western High Plains downstream of an upper level high \ncentered over the Four Corners Region. Near the surface, a \nnearly stalled frontal boundary extends from extreme southeast \nColorado down into western Oklahoma.\n\nAlthough drier conditions are likely across much of southwest \nKansas tonight, there are minimal thunderstorm chances (20%) \nacross central Kansas even with the SREF depicting a weakening \nnorth-northwest flow aloft with the aforementioned upper level \nhigh shifting slightly east out of the Four Corners Region into \nthe Southern Rockies. Near the surface, prevailing east-\nsoutheast upsloping will reinforce pooled moisture north of a \nquasi-stationary frontal boundary to our south, providing modest\ninstability across central Kansas with MUCAPE values upward of \n1000-1500 J/kg. With this setup, thunderstorm development is \nexpected across western/central Nebraska late afternoon/early \nevening with storms then spreading south-southeast into northern\nKansas overnight, potentially clipping our northeastern zones \nnear the I-70 corridor. This is suggested by a few CAMs while \nthe HREF hints at a 10 to 20% probability of 6-hr QPF exceeding \n0.1 of an inch by early Saturday morning. A similar set up \nexists Saturday night into early Sunday morning with little \nchange to the larger scale pattern.\n\nTemperatures are forecast to remain a little above the seasonal \naverage tonight as prevailing east-southeast upsloping does \nlittleto modify the air mass across the region. With the HREF \npointing to a 60 to 80% probability of temperatures dropping \nbelow 70F across much of the area to less than a 10% probability\nof slipping below 70F in south central Kansas, look for lows \nmainly in the 60s(F) overnight with the lower 70s(F) holding on \nin south central Kansas. High temperatures back off a little \nSaturday with prevailing easterlies drawing in slightly cooler \nair into western Kansas, lowering H85 temperatures down into the\nmid-20s(C) in central Kansas to the upper 20s(C) in extreme \nsouthwest Kansas. Expect afternoon highs generally up into the \n90s(F) with the NBM painting a 50 to 70% probability of \ntemperatures exceeding 90F in west central/central Kansas to \nonly a 60 to 80% probability of temperatures even topping 95F in\nsouthwest Kansas into south central Kansas. Similar highs are \nexpected Sunday.",
